1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
From the moment we can walk,

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:05,000
our feet take us everywhere.

3
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
But through the ages, from size to size,

4
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
it's shoes that propel us, protect us,

5
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
and take us places we could never go without them.

6
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:21,000
And this is a packet of fibers that could literally stop a bullet.

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Shoes may seem simple, but don't be fooled.

8
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
They're incredibly complex in design and built with purpose.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,140
From heavy combat boots to the lightest track shoe in the world.

10
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:39,100
We would not be where we are today without shoes.

11
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:43,300
Shoes definitely change what humans are capable of.

12
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:49,580
Slip into our shoes as we reveal what makes them modern marvels.

13
00:00:49,581 --> 00:00:50,440
Whoa!

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Here's an amazing fact.

15
00:01:02,001 --> 00:01:06,000
In 2010, U.S. consumers purchased

16
00:01:06,001 --> 00:01:10,000
2.27 billion pairs of shoes.

17
00:01:10,001 --> 00:01:13,000
That's seven pairs for every person.

18
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:16,000
And the number one selling sizes for men and women?

19
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:19,000
For men, it's a 10 and a half.

20
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:22,000
Women, size eight.

21
00:01:22,001 --> 00:01:25,000
Obviously, shoes are a big part of our lives,

22
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:28,000
bigger than most of us ever realize.

23
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,940
The foot is really our interaction with our world.

24
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:34,460
It's what contacts the world and interacts with it.

25
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:39,100
The average person takes about 7,000 steps per day.

26
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:42,060
And in all the environments where we live and work,

27
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:44,660
we depend on shoes.

28
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:48,660
But there's one place where the right shoe may matter most of all.

29
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:50,780
In combat.

30
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:54,720
A fact our soldiers know all too well.

31
00:01:54,721 --> 00:01:59,720
In the early 2000s, when troops first went to Afghanistan,

32
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:02,960
complaints spread about boots wearing out within a few months.

33
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:07,960
Although the conventional US Army desert boots held up well

34
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:12,840
in the Gulf War, in Afghanistan, they were falling apart.

35
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:15,900
The problem, the soles were designed for sand,

36
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,780
but Afghanistan's desert terrain is different,

37
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:21,520
a rare blend of sand and sharp rocks.

38
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:28,960
To solve the problem, the military turned to Vibram, USA,

39
00:02:28,961 --> 00:02:31,260
a master in rubber soling.

40
00:02:31,261 --> 00:02:34,800
Vibram created a special recipe for a new type of sole

41
00:02:34,801 --> 00:02:38,240
that could handle both types of terrain.

42
00:02:38,241 --> 00:02:40,500
And today, these soles are being produced in bulk.

43
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:47,980
It's all thanks to Vitaly Brahmani, an Italian mountaineer.

44
00:02:47,981 --> 00:02:52,040
In 1936, he invented the first ever rubber-soled shoe.

45
00:02:54,720 --> 00:02:58,120
It was a game changer that created endless possibilities

46
00:02:58,220 --> 00:02:59,420
for what shoes could do.

47
00:03:02,620 --> 00:03:05,160
Today, Vitaly Bramani's company, Vibram,

48
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:10,060
is the largest rubber sole manufacturer in the US.

49
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:12,280
Their factory in North Brookfield, Massachusetts,

50
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:17,180
makes more than 5,000 different styles,

51
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:22,020
from a logger sole that can withstand 18,000 volts

52
00:03:22,021 --> 00:03:27,020
to a firefighter sole that can brave 475 degrees Fahrenheit without melting.

53
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:31,480
Soles keep Bill Ells on his toes.

54
00:03:33,540 --> 00:03:35,780
It's a very complex process.

55
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:39,880
One we'd really like to make simpler, but this is the world of rubber soling.

56
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:43,140
The process begins in the mixing room.

57
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:48,100
Just like the new military sole, each sole has its very own recipe.

58
00:03:48,101 --> 00:03:54,080
A mix of raw rubber, polymers, and a touch of secret additives.

59
00:03:56,380 --> 00:03:59,300
All precisely measured for the demands of each sole.

60
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:02,860
Every batch makes about 500 soles.

61
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:07,860
The ingredients are dumped into a massive computer-controlled mixing machine.

62
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:11,660
Preheated to about 300 degrees Fahrenheit.

63
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:17,320
Using a 17,000 horsepower motor with two sets of teeth,

64
00:04:17,321 --> 00:04:20,120
It mixes each batch in about six minutes.

65
00:04:20,121 --> 00:04:21,820
It's got a chamber in there,

66
00:04:21,821 --> 00:04:24,320
and it's just mashing these two things together.

67
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:30,160
Once mixed, the rubber drops down

68
00:04:30,161 --> 00:04:32,660
onto heavy gauge steel rolls.

69
00:04:32,661 --> 00:04:34,660
Like a dough roller on steroids,

70
00:04:34,661 --> 00:04:36,660
it continues to blend the ingredients

71
00:04:36,661 --> 00:04:39,320
until everything is evenly dispersed.

72
00:04:39,321 --> 00:04:41,320
We can't have a batch that's got a little bit of this

73
00:04:41,321 --> 00:04:42,820
and a little bit of that.

74
00:04:42,821 --> 00:04:46,320
It needs to be even throughout the whole batch.

75
00:04:46,321 --> 00:04:49,320
From there, the mixture moves to a modified gear pump,

76
00:04:49,420 --> 00:04:53,360
which shapes the rubber into rectangles called preforms.

77
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:55,860
Preforms are the soles in raw form.

78
00:04:58,060 --> 00:05:00,660
Inside the machine, sheets of rubber

79
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:02,460
meet a rotating screw.

80
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:04,380
The screw cuts up the rubber, which

81
00:05:04,480 --> 00:05:06,780
fills the void around it.

82
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:10,180
Then gears, using 20,000 pounds of pressure,

83
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:12,020
force the material through an opening

84
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:13,680
in the shape of the desired preform.

85
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:19,320
Not only does every preform have its own size,

86
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:22,820
but if it's a seven wide or a seven narrow,

87
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:24,820
that's got its own preform.

88
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:26,320
So you can just do the multiplication.

89
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:31,660
To become an outsole, every preform

90
00:05:31,760 --> 00:05:35,780
is first precooked in an industrial microwave.

91
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:39,080
Then it's placed in a mold and enters an oven press,

92
00:05:39,180 --> 00:05:41,580
where it cooks at 375 degrees.

93
00:05:41,581 --> 00:05:44,580
degrees.

94
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:47,380
10 minutes later, hot off the press,

95
00:05:47,480 --> 00:05:50,060
one of the US military's latest and greatest soles

96
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:53,060
for Afghanistan.

97
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:57,300
Just a few final trimmings, and it's complete.

98
00:05:57,400 --> 00:06:00,360
The shoe I'm holding in my hand is the very first,

99
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:02,860
one of the very first production shoes only made

100
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:05,100
about 10 days ago.

101
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:07,940
Specially designed for traction and durability,

102
00:06:08,040 --> 00:06:11,580
within weeks, these soles will be on the feet of US soldiers

103
00:06:11,581 --> 00:06:12,580
in Afghanistan.

104
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:20,420
Today, Vibram produces soles for just about every job,

105
00:06:20,520 --> 00:06:23,960
from different varieties of combat boots

106
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:25,900
to specialized work boots for hundreds

107
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,040
of demanding occupations.

108
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:31,060
But none of this would have been possible

109
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:36,000
without one man's discovery, that of Charles Goodyear.

110
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:38,800
Goodyear didn't invent rubber, but he figured out how

111
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:41,580
to use it in a new way.

112
00:06:41,581 --> 00:06:44,420
Natural rubber was discovered by ancient civilizations

113
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:47,020
like the Maya and the Aztecs.

114
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:48,820
They collected sap from rubber trees

115
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:54,320
and used it to create all sorts of products, including shoes.

116
00:06:54,420 --> 00:06:57,020
But natural rubber has its limitations.

117
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:00,040
It hardens in cold weather and melts in hot.

118
00:07:04,140 --> 00:07:07,040
What Charles Goodyear did was discover a chemical process,

119
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:09,240
which adds sulfur to natural rubber

120
00:07:09,241 --> 00:07:13,740
to create a durable, waterproof, and flexible material.

121
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:16,380
His process, called vulcanization,

122
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:19,980
forever changed the quality of our soles, our shoes,

123
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:21,680
and our lives.

124
00:07:21,780 --> 00:07:24,280
There's no way we could have gotten into many environments

125
00:07:24,380 --> 00:07:27,300
and do many of the things that we do without shoes.

126
00:07:27,400 --> 00:07:29,300
But when did shoes first appear?

127
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:34,300
The Romans were one of the earliest cultures

128
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:35,800
to create a variety of footwear.

129
00:07:35,801 --> 00:07:42,100
of footwear, from sandals with leather straps

130
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:44,600
to closed-toed hobnailed shoes for soldiers.

131
00:07:47,180 --> 00:07:51,120
But the oldest evidence of shoes is a sagebrush bark sandal

132
00:07:51,220 --> 00:07:54,060
found in Fort Rock Cave, Oregon.

133
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:58,560
It dates back to around 10,000 years ago.

134
00:07:58,660 --> 00:08:01,800
But another theory pushes the date back even further.

135
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:03,500
Early humans must have worn shoes

136
00:08:03,501 --> 00:08:05,740
because humans were starting to populate

137
00:08:05,840 --> 00:08:11,100
northern and eastern Siberia and managed to cross into Alaska

138
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:14,940
and populate the Americas by at least 15,000 years ago.

139
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:16,640
And those people had to have worn shoes

140
00:08:16,740 --> 00:08:18,940
in such a cold climate.

141
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:20,580
But Dr. Richmond believes shoes

142
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:22,920
first appeared even earlier.

143
00:08:23,020 --> 00:08:27,060
There's some evidence just from the bones in the feet

144
00:08:27,160 --> 00:08:31,200
that shoes go back to at least 30,000 years ago.

145
00:08:31,201 --> 00:08:36,200
The evidence lies in the bones of ancient toes.

146
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:40,140
Analysis of human remains from 30,000 years ago

147
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:45,240
revealed that toe bones began to become thinner.

148
00:08:45,340 --> 00:08:48,160
No other part of the skeleton showed thinning bones.

149
00:08:48,260 --> 00:08:50,760
It was only the toes.

150
00:08:50,860 --> 00:08:53,720
So that suggests that those early humans,

151
00:08:53,820 --> 00:08:57,360
about 30,000 years ago, started to grow slightly less strong

152
00:08:57,460 --> 00:09:00,020
bones because they're wearing shoes.

153
00:09:00,021 --> 00:09:02,820
So it appears that wearing shoes throughout our lives

154
00:09:02,920 --> 00:09:04,820
actually affects the growth of our bones.

155
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:10,860
Shoes protect our feet and enable us

156
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:13,960
to enter a variety of environments.

157
00:09:14,060 --> 00:09:17,140
But as we saw with combat boots, purpose-built shoes

158
00:09:17,240 --> 00:09:20,800
make us capable of even more.

159
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:25,780
The entire shoe fits, not just the foot, but the job.

160
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:29,720
Construction is one of the largest industries in the US.

161
00:09:29,721 --> 00:09:31,720
It employs about 5 million people.

162
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:34,800
It's also one of the most dangerous.

163
00:09:37,220 --> 00:09:41,640
In 2010, some 800 construction workers died on the job.

164
00:09:44,020 --> 00:09:46,640
The number one cause falls.

165
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:51,220
Flip resistance is a great concern to us.

166
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:56,780
Crushed toes and electric shock also account for thousands of construction injuries every year.

167
00:09:56,781 --> 00:10:05,380
But the right shoe can change it all.

168
00:10:05,381 --> 00:10:10,140
Red Wing Shoe Company, located in Red Wing, Minnesota, has been making shoes for more than

169
00:10:10,141 --> 00:10:12,940
a hundred years.

170
00:10:12,941 --> 00:10:19,040
We build purpose-built, functional, quality work shoes.

171
00:10:19,041 --> 00:10:22,460
Here there's a shoe fit for every job.

172
00:10:22,461 --> 00:10:24,920
builds more than 20,000 different combinations

173
00:10:25,020 --> 00:10:27,000
of styles, sizes, and widths.

174
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:35,200
No matter the style, it all begins in the cutting room.

175
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:39,520
This is where the leather gets spliced and diced.

176
00:10:39,620 --> 00:10:44,820
Dave Truline has been cutting with the company for 43 years.

177
00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:48,120
And when it comes to hides, he's the expert.

178
00:10:48,220 --> 00:10:49,920
Physically, different parts of the shoe

179
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:52,460
require different physical properties of the leather.

180
00:10:52,461 --> 00:10:56,460
Like wood, leather has a grain to it, but it varies.

181
00:10:56,560 --> 00:10:59,060
It's much tighter and firmer along the back

182
00:10:59,160 --> 00:11:03,200
compared to the belly, which is loose and soft.

183
00:11:03,300 --> 00:11:06,200
From a leather standpoint, the most critical part of a shoe

184
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:08,000
is the toe.

185
00:11:08,100 --> 00:11:12,020
That's because it often takes the most wear.

186
00:11:12,120 --> 00:11:16,220
So the leather for the van is cut from the back of the hide,

187
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:19,120
while internal components of the shoe, like the tongue,

188
00:11:19,220 --> 00:11:22,460
are cut from the belly.

189
00:11:22,461 --> 00:11:27,460
Using large cutters called dies, workers chop up the leather.

190
00:11:31,460 --> 00:11:34,460
Six key pieces will ultimately form a shoe.

191
00:11:37,460 --> 00:11:40,460
From the cutting room, it's on to the pre-fit department.

192
00:11:41,460 --> 00:11:44,460
Here, the upper part of the shoe starts to take shape.

193
00:11:47,460 --> 00:11:49,460
Seams are the weakest part of a boot.

194
00:11:49,461 --> 00:11:50,720
of a boot.

195
00:11:50,820 --> 00:11:52,960
To combat the problem, Red Wing turns

196
00:11:53,060 --> 00:11:57,460
to a hardy workhorse, the Puritan Stitch Machine.

197
00:11:57,560 --> 00:12:00,840
The Puritan Stitch is one of the signature details

198
00:12:00,940 --> 00:12:03,780
of the construction of a Red Wing boot.

199
00:12:03,880 --> 00:12:06,680
The machine was invented in 1893,

200
00:12:06,780 --> 00:12:09,680
and it hasn't changed a bit.

201
00:12:09,780 --> 00:12:13,860
Its secret weapon is a unique triple stitch using thread

202
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:17,460
that has been pre-dipped in wax.

203
00:12:17,461 --> 00:12:20,660
When the wax dries, it locks the stitches tight,

204
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:24,200
making the seam strong and durable.

205
00:12:24,300 --> 00:12:26,200
This means when you move in your shoes,

206
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:28,700
the stitches on the shoe will hold strong,

207
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:32,700
regardless of your size or shape.

208
00:12:32,800 --> 00:12:34,120
Here's a surprise.

209
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:38,020
Can you imagine not having a right and left shoe?

210
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:40,220
That's how it used to be.

211
00:12:40,320 --> 00:12:44,520
Before 1850, right and left shoes were both the same,

212
00:12:44,620 --> 00:12:46,560
and breaking them in to fit your feet

213
00:12:46,561 --> 00:12:48,320
wasn't easy.

214
00:12:48,420 --> 00:12:52,800
Fortunately for us, that's not the case today.

215
00:12:52,900 --> 00:12:55,560
The forms that determine the size, shape, and width

216
00:12:55,660 --> 00:12:59,300
of each shoe are called lasts.

217
00:12:59,400 --> 00:13:02,900
And today, they come in both right and left shapes.

218
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,920
Lasts are company secrets.

219
00:13:06,020 --> 00:13:09,920
Shoe manufacturers worldwide carefully guard their own.

220
00:13:10,020 --> 00:13:13,120
The next step in the process is to attach the shoe's upper

221
00:13:13,220 --> 00:13:15,820
onto a last.

222
00:13:15,821 --> 00:13:18,920
Workers steam the leather into the shapes of both right and

223
00:13:19,020 --> 00:13:19,720
left lasts.

224
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:27,760
Then it's on to the next step, getting purpose-built.

225
00:13:27,860 --> 00:13:32,240
For steel-toe boots, the worker peels back the leather, and with

226
00:13:32,340 --> 00:13:34,480
a little bit of glue, heat, and pressure,

227
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:38,180
inserts the steel cap.

228
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:41,620
Other styles get different forms of protection.

229
00:13:41,720 --> 00:13:44,620
Just like the steel toe is protecting toes,

230
00:13:44,621 --> 00:13:48,220
the plate is protecting the bottom of the worker's feet.

231
00:13:48,320 --> 00:13:52,860
The worker that's wearing it is able to step on sharp objects,

232
00:13:52,960 --> 00:13:56,860
and it's going to protect the soles of their feet.

233
00:13:56,960 --> 00:14:01,000
Now it's time for bottoming, the final step.

234
00:14:01,100 --> 00:14:05,100
A special slip-resistant mix of urethane squirts into a mold.

235
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:11,140
Then the worker attaches a completed upper

236
00:14:11,141 --> 00:14:14,440
and sends it through the drying tunnel.

237
00:14:14,540 --> 00:14:17,880
Within minutes, the sole hardens.

238
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:24,080
A robot lifts out the completed shoe,

239
00:14:24,180 --> 00:14:29,000
and then it's off to inspection and packaging.

240
00:14:29,100 --> 00:14:33,300
The boot is now ready to wear and protect its wearer.

241
00:14:33,400 --> 00:14:35,300
We put a lot into a work boot,

242
00:14:35,400 --> 00:14:38,200
and we try to build a perfect work boot.

243
00:14:41,140 --> 00:14:44,380
Red Wing knows that workers' lives depend on the boots they

244
00:14:44,480 --> 00:14:45,380
make.

245
00:14:45,480 --> 00:14:48,440
But there's also another line of defense.

246
00:14:48,540 --> 00:14:50,880
Meet Pam Kowalewski.

247
00:14:50,980 --> 00:14:54,380
She holds manufacturers' feet to the fire.

248
00:14:54,480 --> 00:14:57,380
As a personal protective equipment engineer at Intertech

249
00:14:57,480 --> 00:15:00,800
Testing in Cortland, New York, it's her job to confirm

250
00:15:00,900 --> 00:15:04,360
purpose-built shoes hold up to their claims and meet industry

251
00:15:04,460 --> 00:15:06,440
standards.

252
00:15:06,540 --> 00:15:08,900
People might be surprised that so much testing goes

253
00:15:08,901 --> 00:15:10,500
that so much testing goes in the shoes,

254
00:15:10,501 --> 00:15:12,700
but you have to think about the life of a firefighter

255
00:15:12,701 --> 00:15:17,200
or any first responder and the hazards that they see every day.

256
00:15:17,201 --> 00:15:20,840
Every month, Pam tests 30 industry shoes.

257
00:15:20,841 --> 00:15:25,580
She cuts them, burns them, and beats them

258
00:15:25,581 --> 00:15:27,820
to the point of breaking.

259
00:15:27,821 --> 00:15:31,360
Today's victim for the torture tests, a firefighter's boot.

260
00:15:38,900 --> 00:15:41,360
On the front lines, firefighters need protection.

261
00:15:42,240 --> 00:15:44,280
And historically, they didn't have it.

262
00:15:46,680 --> 00:15:48,600
Protective gear used to be so minimal

263
00:15:48,601 --> 00:15:51,280
that it was virtually impossible to enter burning buildings.

264
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:56,880
As a result, fires were often fought solely from the outside,

265
00:15:57,220 --> 00:15:59,080
and many structures burned to the ground.

266
00:16:02,220 --> 00:16:04,560
It wasn't until after World War II

267
00:16:04,561 --> 00:16:07,160
that standards for firefighting gear were developed.

268
00:16:07,161 --> 00:16:11,160
Today's firefighter boots are required to protect on the job,

269
00:16:11,260 --> 00:16:13,660
both near a fire and in it.

270
00:16:16,600 --> 00:16:19,800
And that's what Pam is about to test.

271
00:16:19,900 --> 00:16:23,180
So what we have here is what's known as the TPP apparatus,

272
00:16:23,280 --> 00:16:26,740
and the TPP stands for thermal protective performance.

273
00:16:26,840 --> 00:16:28,940
That means how well a material can protect

274
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:31,580
against blistering heat.

275
00:16:31,680 --> 00:16:34,980
The TPP rating is based on the amount of time before a person

276
00:16:34,981 --> 00:16:39,220
before a person would receive second-degree burns.

277
00:16:39,221 --> 00:16:41,120
This is an important test for firefighters

278
00:16:41,121 --> 00:16:43,020
because you want to keep their core temperature

279
00:16:43,021 --> 00:16:46,520
as low as possible, so you want a very high TPP rating

280
00:16:46,521 --> 00:16:49,420
to keep the heat out of the garment.

281
00:16:49,421 --> 00:16:51,420
To test the firefighter boot,

282
00:16:51,421 --> 00:16:53,540
Pam places a piece of the upper part of the boot

283
00:16:53,541 --> 00:16:58,840
in the machine and puts a copper calorimeter on top.

284
00:16:58,841 --> 00:17:00,540
This monitors the heat of the material

285
00:17:00,541 --> 00:17:04,440
when placed in the flame.

286
00:17:04,441 --> 00:17:07,880
It simulates what firefighters face every time they battle a

287
00:17:07,881 --> 00:17:09,280
blaze.

288
00:17:09,281 --> 00:17:10,880
So let's see what the sample looks like.

289
00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:17,380
You can see some of the char marks on the outer shell.

290
00:17:17,381 --> 00:17:19,280
This would be the moisture barrier.

291
00:17:19,281 --> 00:17:21,500
And this is the thermal liner.

292
00:17:21,501 --> 00:17:25,160
The computer analyzes the data and gives it a TPP time

293
00:17:25,161 --> 00:17:30,640
rating of 21.9, which means a fireman wearing this boot

294
00:17:30,641 --> 00:17:33,700
would have about 22 seconds before getting second degree

295
00:17:33,701 --> 00:17:37,500
The 22 seconds for this particular composite

296
00:17:37,600 --> 00:17:40,180
means that the firefighter would have enough chance

297
00:17:40,280 --> 00:17:44,340
that they could escape safely and not suffer severe burns.

298
00:17:46,280 --> 00:17:49,780
Burns aren't the only hazard firefighters face.

299
00:17:49,880 --> 00:17:53,220
Falling objects, rusty nails, collapsed structures,

300
00:17:53,320 --> 00:17:55,060
the list goes on.

301
00:17:55,160 --> 00:17:57,060
And the last thing you want is your toes

302
00:17:57,160 --> 00:17:59,800
to end up looking like this.

303
00:17:59,900 --> 00:18:02,400
Steel-toe boots are designed to prevent

304
00:18:02,401 --> 00:18:07,779
boots are designed to prevent that if they pass the impact test.

305
00:18:07,780 --> 00:18:10,300
So the idea behind the test is

306
00:18:10,301 --> 00:18:15,940
that you would put a cylinder of clay plasticine inside the steel toe, come down with an impactor,

307
00:18:15,941 --> 00:18:22,120
which is 50 pounds, and then after the test we'll pull out the plasticine to see if it is shrunk

308
00:18:22,121 --> 00:18:24,212
down to at least a half an inch.

309
00:18:24,213 --> 00:18:28,200
If it's lower than a half an inch it would fail the test. Pam

310
00:18:28,201 --> 00:18:30,240
saws off the front of a steel toe boot

311
00:18:30,241 --> 00:18:37,300
and inserts a clay cylinder to simulate toes.

312
00:18:39,220 --> 00:18:41,120
Then, using a 50-pound weight,

313
00:18:41,800 --> 00:18:44,780
she smashes a steel dumbbell straight down on the toe.

314
00:18:46,400 --> 00:18:48,540
The toe looks to be in great shape,

315
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:50,520
no visible damage,

316
00:18:50,840 --> 00:18:53,600
and now we'll measure the height of the plasticine cylinder.

317
00:18:54,240 --> 00:18:55,980
It passes with flying colors.

318
00:18:55,981 --> 00:18:59,780
So Modern Marvels decides to up the attic.

319
00:19:01,160 --> 00:19:03,360
Could a boot actually protect an egg?

320
00:19:04,380 --> 00:19:05,580
We're going to find out.

321
00:19:06,140 --> 00:19:08,840
In a non-steel-toe boot, there's no chance.

322
00:19:10,320 --> 00:19:11,920
The egg splatters everywhere.

323
00:19:13,520 --> 00:19:15,100
But the steel-toe boot?

324
00:19:16,220 --> 00:19:18,180
I don't know if this is going to work or not.

325
00:19:19,680 --> 00:19:23,180
Pam inserts the egg, readies the level, and releases.

326
00:19:23,181 --> 00:19:25,180
Whoa!

327
00:19:25,181 --> 00:19:30,180
Surprisingly, the egg survives, with just a slight crack.

328
00:19:30,181 --> 00:19:33,180
I am very impressed.

329
00:19:33,181 --> 00:19:36,180
I would feel safe in this shoe.

330
00:19:36,181 --> 00:19:39,180
The technology has changed so much throughout the years

331
00:19:39,181 --> 00:19:42,180
that now this allows a firefighter to be safer

332
00:19:42,181 --> 00:19:44,180
than what we had in the past.

333
00:19:48,180 --> 00:19:51,180
Purpose-built shoes are carefully designed

334
00:19:51,181 --> 00:19:55,420
carefully designed and tested to the max.

335
00:19:55,421 --> 00:19:59,760
But the true worth of a shoe is not how it performs in the lab,

336
00:19:59,761 --> 00:20:00,461
but on the job.

337
00:20:03,400 --> 00:20:08,060
No worker is more defined by shoes than the cowboy.

338
00:20:08,061 --> 00:20:12,740
From wrangling herds to racing over harsh terrain,

339
00:20:12,741 --> 00:20:16,240
cowboy boots get a workout.

340
00:20:16,241 --> 00:20:20,180
And though today we may think of them as high style,

341
00:20:20,181 --> 00:20:24,080
To a cowboy, they're nearly as important as his horse.

342
00:20:24,081 --> 00:20:28,320
For a cowboy, a pair of boots is another part of his tools.

343
00:20:28,321 --> 00:20:32,840
Everything that we have is we use for protection.

344
00:20:32,841 --> 00:20:35,500
Farming and ranching currently ranks number four

345
00:20:35,501 --> 00:20:39,160
as the most dangerous job in the US.

346
00:20:39,161 --> 00:20:42,340
It's not easy herding cattle.

347
00:20:42,341 --> 00:20:44,640
If the cowboy falls off his horse,

348
00:20:44,641 --> 00:20:48,980
he risks getting trampled or even killed.

349
00:20:48,981 --> 00:20:50,640
That's where the cowboy boot comes in.

350
00:20:53,840 --> 00:20:55,700
Every single feature of its construction

351
00:20:55,701 --> 00:20:58,660
is designed to help the cowboy do his job safely.

352
00:21:00,180 --> 00:21:03,900
A tall heel prevents the foot from sliding through the stirrup

353
00:21:03,901 --> 00:21:05,740
and helps keep the rider on the horse.

354
00:21:07,140 --> 00:21:10,460
Smooth leather soles ensure easy mounting and dismounting

355
00:21:10,461 --> 00:21:11,580
in and out of the stirrup.

356
00:21:12,780 --> 00:21:16,100
We use a leather sole on ours and it's smoother,

357
00:21:16,101 --> 00:21:17,480
slides in a little easier,

358
00:21:17,960 --> 00:21:20,460
and it doesn't have that sticking effect

359
00:21:20,461 --> 00:21:22,060
as you're going in and out of the stirrups.

360
00:21:23,100 --> 00:21:25,000
Tall shafts protect the lower leg

361
00:21:25,001 --> 00:21:26,340
from rubbing on the saddle leathers

362
00:21:26,341 --> 00:21:29,360
and guard against brush and thorns.

363
00:21:30,840 --> 00:21:33,000
Clips on the heel keep spurs in place.

364
00:21:34,220 --> 00:21:36,340
Even the decorative stitching has a purpose.

365
00:21:37,400 --> 00:21:39,080
The stitches hold the boot up

366
00:21:39,081 --> 00:21:40,820
and prevent the leather from collapsing.

367
00:21:41,820 --> 00:21:43,260
Without the cowboy boots,

368
00:21:43,580 --> 00:21:45,380
it would make my job a little more difficult.

369
00:21:46,100 --> 00:21:50,100
Hey, come on, girls. Come on.

370
00:21:50,101 --> 00:21:53,100
Cowboy boots go back to the 16th century

371
00:21:53,101 --> 00:21:57,100
and the first cowboys, the Spanish vaqueros.

372
00:21:57,101 --> 00:21:59,100
Originally, the boots were handmade

373
00:21:59,101 --> 00:22:02,100
with plain brown or black leather.

374
00:22:02,101 --> 00:22:06,100
It wasn't until the era of the great cattle drives

375
00:22:06,101 --> 00:22:10,100
of the 1870s and 80s that boots went big.

376
00:22:10,101 --> 00:22:14,100
Cowboys fresh off the trail would roll into town

377
00:22:14,101 --> 00:22:16,500
would roll into town with hot money in their pockets.

378
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:23,440
New boots became a status symbol, so unique designs

379
00:22:23,540 --> 00:22:25,440
and topstitching evolved to meet the market.

380
00:22:29,340 --> 00:22:33,660
Today, no one knows the trends like Tony Llama boots.

381
00:22:33,760 --> 00:22:37,260
Llama himself founded the company in 1911 in El Paso,

382
00:22:37,360 --> 00:22:38,060
Texas.

383
00:22:40,760 --> 00:22:42,860
He ran it for 63 years.

384
00:22:42,861 --> 00:22:46,100
And today, his tradition of quality handcrafted boots

385
00:22:46,200 --> 00:22:47,100
lives on.

386
00:22:49,600 --> 00:22:51,660
To celebrate their 100th anniversary,

387
00:22:51,760 --> 00:22:57,200
the firm recently made the most expensive boots ever,

388
00:22:57,300 --> 00:23:02,220
a $50,000 pair of American alligator belly boots,

389
00:23:02,320 --> 00:23:05,820
each set with 100 diamonds and four ounces of gold.

390
00:23:08,960 --> 00:23:12,860
Most of the boots, however, range from 150 to 1,200 pounds.

391
00:23:12,861 --> 00:23:15,900
And there's big demand.

392
00:23:16,960 --> 00:23:20,500
Every year, this factory makes about 100,000 pairs of boots,

393
00:23:21,040 --> 00:23:24,740
each of which requires about 20 square feet of leather.

394
00:23:27,840 --> 00:23:29,980
And if you want to take a walk on the wild side,

395
00:23:31,280 --> 00:23:33,580
here you can get just about any kind of leather.

396
00:23:34,840 --> 00:23:37,600
Here are all our exotic leathers that we use here.

397
00:23:37,601 --> 00:23:40,160
There's ostrich, elephant, shark.

398
00:23:40,780 --> 00:23:42,400
We have lizard, python.

399
00:23:42,860 --> 00:23:46,860
caiman, and stingray.

400
00:23:46,861 --> 00:23:48,860
But it's going to cost you.

401
00:23:48,861 --> 00:23:51,860
Whether it's the quill on the ostrich

402
00:23:51,861 --> 00:23:53,860
or the scales on the python,

403
00:23:53,861 --> 00:23:56,860
the parts need to be mirror images.

404
00:23:56,861 --> 00:23:58,860
And it takes time.

405
00:23:58,861 --> 00:24:01,860
These exotic skins are difficult to match.

406
00:24:01,861 --> 00:24:04,860
It's really an art to be an exotic cutter.

407
00:24:04,861 --> 00:24:07,860
And the reason it is because you have to go through

408
00:24:07,861 --> 00:24:11,860
300 or 400 pairs of skins to match them exactly.

409
00:24:12,860 --> 00:24:17,500
Most boots, however, are made of plain old cow hides.

410
00:24:17,600 --> 00:24:19,160
But getting leather in a workable form

411
00:24:19,260 --> 00:24:24,000
is a process in and of itself called tanning.

412
00:24:24,100 --> 00:24:27,000
SB Foot Tanning Company in Red Wing, Minnesota,

413
00:24:27,100 --> 00:24:30,120
is the largest tannery in the US.

414
00:24:30,220 --> 00:24:31,920
20 million square feet of leather

415
00:24:32,020 --> 00:24:33,220
run through here every year.

416
00:24:37,160 --> 00:24:40,920
The hides come in as what's called wet blues, which

417
00:24:40,921 --> 00:24:43,060
which means they've already been de-haired

418
00:24:43,061 --> 00:24:46,360
and treated with chromium to prevent against bacterial infection.

419
00:24:47,400 --> 00:24:49,560
Now they've got to be dyed and dried.

420
00:24:51,220 --> 00:24:53,320
First up, the color mills.

421
00:24:54,400 --> 00:24:58,120
17 massive drums soak the hides in a mix of dyes and chemicals.

422
00:25:00,380 --> 00:25:04,760
Depending on the color, the drums run anywhere from 6 to 16 hours.

423
00:25:05,420 --> 00:25:08,500
The blue goes in, it comes out whatever color we wanted to make it,

424
00:25:08,501 --> 00:25:10,380
yellow, blues, browns, blacks.

425
00:25:10,920 --> 00:25:13,260
You make almost every color under the rainbow here.

426
00:25:16,060 --> 00:25:19,560
The sopping wet hides then move to a massive vacuum dryer

427
00:25:19,660 --> 00:25:23,260
that sucks out most of the water,

428
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:25,060
while air takes care of the rest.

429
00:25:28,180 --> 00:25:31,280
To enhance the final colors, the hides

430
00:25:31,380 --> 00:25:32,780
go through a waterfall of oil.

431
00:25:35,680 --> 00:25:39,580
Then a spray booth applies a top coat,

432
00:25:39,581 --> 00:25:43,080
and infrared heaters set the finish.

433
00:25:43,081 --> 00:25:45,120
This is what we have for a final product,

434
00:25:45,121 --> 00:25:48,320
a nice, durable, strong piece of leather.

435
00:25:48,321 --> 00:25:49,920
These finely tanned leather hides

436
00:25:49,921 --> 00:25:54,020
will ship all over the US to high-end shoemakers,

437
00:25:54,021 --> 00:25:55,220
just like Tony Llama.

438
00:25:59,440 --> 00:26:02,740
Whether it's exotic leather or plain cowhide,

439
00:26:02,741 --> 00:26:07,340
this company can make your boots as elaborate as you want.

440
00:26:07,341 --> 00:26:13,820
For special orders, workers hand cut, hand stitch,

441
00:26:13,920 --> 00:26:17,180
and hand thread the designs.

442
00:26:17,280 --> 00:26:20,280
It's a tedious process.

443
00:26:20,380 --> 00:26:24,360
Most stitch patterns require 5,000 to 16,000 stitches

444
00:26:24,460 --> 00:26:26,820
per upper.

445
00:26:26,920 --> 00:26:28,660
To meet the high demand, the company

446
00:26:28,760 --> 00:26:33,000
relies on automated embroidery machines.

447
00:26:33,100 --> 00:26:35,360
These machines have revolutionized

448
00:26:35,361 --> 00:26:37,120
the stitching part of our business.

449
00:26:38,660 --> 00:26:41,860
About 90% of their boots are now stitched by machine.

450
00:26:43,460 --> 00:26:45,480
Each station has 24 heads

451
00:26:45,481 --> 00:26:48,980
and a needle that can operate at 1,500 stitches per minute.

452
00:26:51,280 --> 00:26:52,820
With the help of these machines,

453
00:26:53,080 --> 00:26:56,000
the average cowboy boot can be made in just three weeks,

454
00:26:56,740 --> 00:27:00,620
which is a lot faster than the two months that it takes

455
00:27:00,621 --> 00:27:02,220
to make most special-order boots.

456
00:27:05,360 --> 00:27:14,080
Perhaps one day these boots will protect a cowboy herding cattle.

457
00:27:14,081 --> 00:27:18,980
Or maybe they'll just be worn for fashion.

458
00:27:18,981 --> 00:27:24,020
But no matter what, you can take cowboy boots out of the Wild West, but you can't take the

459
00:27:24,021 --> 00:27:30,360
Wild West out of cowboy boots.

460
00:27:30,361 --> 00:27:33,260
can open the door to new environments,

461
00:27:33,261 --> 00:27:38,460
protect on the job, and further our performance.

462
00:27:38,461 --> 00:27:41,300
But only if the shoe fits.

463
00:27:41,301 --> 00:27:45,900
There's nothing worse than an ill-fitting pair of shoes.

464
00:27:45,901 --> 00:27:48,620
That's why you've got to get your feet measured

465
00:27:48,621 --> 00:27:51,140
on that little silver thingamajig.

466
00:27:51,141 --> 00:27:54,720
You know, the one you see in every shoe store?

467
00:27:54,721 --> 00:28:00,360
You may not have realized it has a name, the Brannock device.

468
00:28:00,361 --> 00:28:05,360
It was invented in 1927 by Charles Branach.

469
00:28:05,361 --> 00:28:08,360
Not only does the device measure length,

470
00:28:08,361 --> 00:28:10,360
it also accounts for width and arch length

471
00:28:10,361 --> 00:28:13,360
in computing the proper size.

472
00:28:13,361 --> 00:28:17,360
It can fit the foot to within 95% accuracy.

473
00:28:17,361 --> 00:28:20,360
Before the Branach device, people just used plain wooden

474
00:28:20,361 --> 00:28:23,360
rulers to measure feet.

475
00:28:23,361 --> 00:28:26,360
The device was a huge step forward,

476
00:28:26,361 --> 00:28:29,360
and the shoe industry took notice.

477
00:28:29,361 --> 00:28:34,360
Within 10 years, Branick had sold more than 30,000 of his inventions.

478
00:28:34,361 --> 00:28:37,365
The device became so hot that the U.S.

479
00:28:37,366 --> 00:28:41,240
Army commissioned the Branick Company to properly

480
00:28:41,241 --> 00:28:44,040
size soldiers during World War II.

481
00:28:44,041 --> 00:28:50,960
Today, more than 1.5 million Branick devices have been sold worldwide, and it continues

482
00:28:50,961 --> 00:28:55,040
to be the standard measuring tool in the shoe industry.

483
00:28:55,041 --> 00:29:00,140
But now, there's new competition, the i-STEP.

484
00:29:00,141 --> 00:29:02,640
It's the latest innovation in foot measuring,

485
00:29:02,641 --> 00:29:05,320
developed by Atrex.

486
00:29:05,321 --> 00:29:07,580
Using state-of-the-art technology,

487
00:29:07,581 --> 00:29:10,120
this new device precisely measures a foot based

488
00:29:10,121 --> 00:29:15,360
on three key elements, foot length, arch type,

489
00:29:15,361 --> 00:29:18,360
and pressure distribution.

490
00:29:18,361 --> 00:29:21,800
37 gold-plated barometric sensors calculate

491
00:29:21,801 --> 00:29:24,460
how your foot carries weight.

492
00:29:24,461 --> 00:29:28,100
while 1,300 LED lights size the foot.

493
00:29:28,101 --> 00:29:30,140
It allows us to look at this scan

494
00:29:30,141 --> 00:29:33,060
and directly correlate it to a particular footwear

495
00:29:33,061 --> 00:29:36,380
that might be appropriate for that person's foot type.

496
00:29:36,381 --> 00:29:38,800
But there's more.

497
00:29:38,801 --> 00:29:42,080
By analyzing the pressure distribution on the readout,

498
00:29:42,081 --> 00:29:46,440
salespeople can individualize insoles for shoes.

499
00:29:46,441 --> 00:29:48,720
Where the readout indicates increased pressure,

500
00:29:48,721 --> 00:29:51,320
the device recommends removing certain gel pods

501
00:29:51,321 --> 00:29:53,080
from the insoles.

502
00:29:53,081 --> 00:29:56,380
This creates a cavity and offloads the pressure.

503
00:29:56,381 --> 00:29:59,620
Every insole can be made just for you.

504
00:29:59,621 --> 00:30:02,920
It really allows us to make every shoe unique

505
00:30:02,921 --> 00:30:04,720
to that particular customer.

506
00:30:04,721 --> 00:30:06,860
Insoles are typically generic.

507
00:30:06,861 --> 00:30:09,640
These now are specific to a particular person's foot

508
00:30:09,641 --> 00:30:11,340
and pressure point.

509
00:30:11,341 --> 00:30:14,960
Using technology to properly fit shoes is nothing new,

510
00:30:14,961 --> 00:30:16,480
but it wasn't always good for you.

511
00:30:16,481 --> 00:30:23,480
In the 1940s, X-rays were the common approach.

512
00:30:23,481 --> 00:30:26,480
Donning new shoes, people would step into the machine

513
00:30:26,481 --> 00:30:28,480
and see a fluorescent image of their bones

514
00:30:28,481 --> 00:30:31,480
compared to the outline of their shoes.

515
00:30:31,481 --> 00:30:36,480
But because of radiation, this type of fitting faded fast.

516
00:30:36,481 --> 00:30:41,480
By the 1970s, most states had banned the devices.

517
00:30:41,481 --> 00:30:45,480
It's a far cry from the days of measuring feet

518
00:30:45,481 --> 00:30:47,520
with barleycorns.

519
00:30:47,620 --> 00:30:51,020
Yes, we said barleycorns.

520
00:30:51,120 --> 00:30:56,460
During medieval times, an inch was defined as three barleycorns.

521
00:30:56,560 --> 00:30:58,220
And believe it or not, that's what

522
00:30:58,320 --> 00:31:02,200
became the basis for our current sizing system.

523
00:31:02,300 --> 00:31:05,700
The difference between one shoe size and the next hole size

524
00:31:05,800 --> 00:31:09,240
is one barleycorn, or 1 3rd of an inch,

525
00:31:09,340 --> 00:31:14,100
while the half size is 1 6th of an inch.

526
00:31:14,101 --> 00:31:18,100
However you measure your feet, it pays to do it often.

527
00:31:18,200 --> 00:31:20,200
Many people think that once you're an adult,

528
00:31:20,300 --> 00:31:23,340
your feet stay the same size for life.

529
00:31:23,440 --> 00:31:25,540
That's not true.

530
00:31:25,640 --> 00:31:28,740
As feet age, tendons and ligaments stretch out,

531
00:31:28,840 --> 00:31:32,960
arches lower, and fat padding dwindles,

532
00:31:33,060 --> 00:31:36,060
resulting in longer and wider feet.

533
00:31:36,160 --> 00:31:38,960
They can also change size as you gain and lose weight.

534
00:31:38,961 --> 00:31:42,760
weight.

535
00:31:42,860 --> 00:31:47,160
But a good lace-up shoe can help you go with the ebb and flow.

536
00:31:47,260 --> 00:31:49,340
A shoe is only as good as it fits,

537
00:31:49,440 --> 00:31:52,580
and laces can make it just right.

538
00:31:52,680 --> 00:31:55,480
Hickory Brands Incorporated is one of the largest shoelace

539
00:31:55,580 --> 00:32:00,320
manufacturers in the US, producing more than 60 million

540
00:32:00,420 --> 00:32:02,420
laces every year.

541
00:32:02,520 --> 00:32:07,120
That's enough to go around the earth almost two times.

542
00:32:07,121 --> 00:32:11,460
We have brown laces, boot laces, totally flat laces.

543
00:32:11,660 --> 00:32:12,700
We have oval laces.

544
00:32:12,920 --> 00:32:16,860
We have what we call bubble laces, which is shaped like a sausage.

545
00:32:18,320 --> 00:32:21,060
Making a shoelace begins in the braiding department.

546
00:32:22,460 --> 00:32:28,000
Here, some 800 machines equipped with bobbins move about to weave the laces together.

547
00:32:29,580 --> 00:32:35,500
Depending on the desired thickness, machines operate with anywhere from 8 to 44 spools of yarn.

548
00:32:37,120 --> 00:32:40,720
Like a maypole dance, the spindles constantly rotate,

549
00:32:41,460 --> 00:32:43,900
producing 12 to 15 inches of lace per minute.

550
00:32:45,520 --> 00:32:48,260
There's an even faster method called weaving.

551
00:32:49,400 --> 00:32:52,640
Weaving machines output 36 to 40 inches per minute.

552
00:32:54,980 --> 00:32:57,380
Yarn flows from large looms into the weaver,

553
00:32:58,680 --> 00:33:01,580
which interlaces them back and forth in the blink of an eye.

554
00:33:04,520 --> 00:33:05,600
Here's a good question.

555
00:33:05,601 --> 00:33:08,540
have you ever wondered what the end of a shoelace is called?

556
00:33:09,740 --> 00:33:10,680
Drumroll, please.

557
00:33:12,000 --> 00:33:14,460
The technical term is aglet,

558
00:33:14,780 --> 00:33:18,280
which comes from the Latin word acus, for needle,

559
00:33:18,800 --> 00:33:21,320
a simple little thing that makes a big difference.

560
00:33:22,480 --> 00:33:24,280
The aglet is very important

561
00:33:24,281 --> 00:33:25,820
when it comes to lacing up a shoe.

562
00:33:25,960 --> 00:33:28,200
It's really difficult to get it through the eyelet

563
00:33:28,201 --> 00:33:28,960
without the aglet.

564
00:33:29,760 --> 00:33:31,060
To form the aglets,

565
00:33:31,360 --> 00:33:33,020
workers feed the lace into a machine.

566
00:33:33,021 --> 00:33:44,020
Then, at precisely measured intervals, a plastic strip of acetate encases the lace, and a heat press seals it and cuts it in half.

567
00:33:44,021 --> 00:33:47,020
The shoelace is now complete.

568
00:33:47,021 --> 00:33:50,020
Ready to find a home in a new shoe.

569
00:33:50,021 --> 00:33:53,020
Ready to be tied.

570
00:33:53,021 --> 00:33:54,020
to be tied.

571
00:34:00,260 --> 00:34:02,160
Whether you're in the military,

572
00:34:02,161 --> 00:34:04,360
working on a construction site,

573
00:34:04,361 --> 00:34:06,060
or riding horses,

574
00:34:06,061 --> 00:34:08,260
there's a shoe built for you.

575
00:34:08,261 --> 00:34:10,740
But now, a new movement is challenging

576
00:34:10,741 --> 00:34:14,180
the very idea of shoes.

577
00:34:14,181 --> 00:34:18,580
Is it possible that shoes are actually bad for our feet?

578
00:34:18,581 --> 00:34:19,620
There's a huge debate right now

579
00:34:19,621 --> 00:34:21,620
about whether shoes are even good for us

580
00:34:21,621 --> 00:34:24,540
Because we know that over millions of years,

581
00:34:24,660 --> 00:34:26,760
our foot has evolved in a barefoot setting.

582
00:34:27,660 --> 00:34:31,680
In response, a different type of shoe emerged on the market in 2005.

583
00:34:32,640 --> 00:34:34,640
It's called Five Fingers.

584
00:34:35,520 --> 00:34:38,700
The shoes are designed for your feet and all ten of your toes.

585
00:34:40,100 --> 00:34:43,760
They're based on the belief that your foot should move in a more natural way,

586
00:34:44,840 --> 00:34:46,480
almost as if it were barefoot.

587
00:34:47,360 --> 00:34:49,340
It's all about how our feet move.

588
00:34:51,620 --> 00:34:55,620
Our feet are designed to walk in a very efficient manner.

589
00:34:55,720 --> 00:35:00,360
26 bones, 33 joints, and countless muscles, tendons,

590
00:35:00,460 --> 00:35:03,260
and ligaments make up the foot.

591
00:35:03,360 --> 00:35:06,260
During a step, the foot lands on the heel,

592
00:35:06,360 --> 00:35:10,480
rolls along the outside, and then pushes off on the big toe.

593
00:35:10,580 --> 00:35:12,980
This puts a lot of strain on our feet.

594
00:35:13,080 --> 00:35:15,980
It's also a lot of work for our toes.

595
00:35:16,080 --> 00:35:20,520
Brian Richmond is studying how shoes affect our feet.

596
00:35:20,521 --> 00:35:24,720
He and his colleague are setting up a $25,000 specialized

597
00:35:24,820 --> 00:35:28,560
pressure pad to compare shoes to bare feet.

598
00:35:28,660 --> 00:35:31,260
The first thing we do is calibrate the pressure pad.

599
00:35:31,360 --> 00:35:34,260
First up, barefoot.

600
00:35:34,360 --> 00:35:37,740
Using 4,000 sensors, the pad measures what happens

601
00:35:37,840 --> 00:35:40,780
to a foot during a step.

602
00:35:40,880 --> 00:35:44,580
So what you're able to see is the path of that pressure.

603
00:35:44,680 --> 00:35:47,180
So the heel strikes the pad.

604
00:35:47,280 --> 00:35:50,520
Then all the pressure goes onto the outside of the foot.

605
00:35:50,521 --> 00:35:53,160
And you can see how the pressure then zooms over

606
00:35:53,260 --> 00:35:56,760
to the big toe right at toe off.

607
00:35:56,860 --> 00:36:00,500
Walking barefoot creates defined localized stress.

608
00:36:00,600 --> 00:36:03,800
But that's not the case when you wear shoes.

609
00:36:03,900 --> 00:36:05,800
You can see that the pressure is distributed

610
00:36:05,900 --> 00:36:09,000
across the heel more evenly than when someone strikes

611
00:36:09,100 --> 00:36:11,080
with their barefoot heel.

612
00:36:11,180 --> 00:36:14,640
Then the forefoot of the shoe hits.

613
00:36:14,740 --> 00:36:16,920
And here you also see the pressure distribution

614
00:36:17,020 --> 00:36:20,220
is much more evenly distributed across the forefoot

615
00:36:20,221 --> 00:36:23,920
and the toes than what you see in a barefoot walker.

616
00:36:23,921 --> 00:36:27,560
Shoes distribute the stresses across the foot.

617
00:36:27,561 --> 00:36:30,660
So what does that mean for you if you wear shoes?

618
00:36:30,661 --> 00:36:33,460
That means that your foot also doesn't have to work as hard.

619
00:36:33,461 --> 00:36:35,260
Your toes don't have to grip as much,

620
00:36:35,261 --> 00:36:38,280
and they don't have as much work to do.

621
00:36:38,281 --> 00:36:40,880
There's no doubt that wearing shoes protect your feet.

622
00:36:40,881 --> 00:36:43,180
So in that sense, shoes are good for you.

623
00:36:43,181 --> 00:36:46,320
But there is some debate about whether our feet might grow

624
00:36:46,321 --> 00:36:49,280
more naturally and stronger if we were barefoot

625
00:36:49,281 --> 00:36:51,920
when we were growing up.

626
00:36:52,020 --> 00:36:54,080
Minimalist shoes allow the muscles in your feet

627
00:36:54,180 --> 00:36:56,320
to be more stimulated.

628
00:36:56,420 --> 00:37:01,020
So experts think they're the next best thing to bare feet.

629
00:37:01,120 --> 00:37:05,300
Some shoes, however, go beyond what our feet can do.

630
00:37:05,400 --> 00:37:08,400
And they're setting new standards.

631
00:37:08,500 --> 00:37:13,440
It all started in 1936 at the Summer Olympics in Berlin,

632
00:37:13,540 --> 00:37:16,640
when US athlete Jesse Owens took home four gold medals.

633
00:37:19,280 --> 00:37:22,620
Owens was wearing a new type of shoe with spikes,

634
00:37:22,720 --> 00:37:26,880
which gave him better traction and enabled him to run faster.

635
00:37:26,980 --> 00:37:30,620
Adi Dasler was the ingenious inventor of these spike shoes

636
00:37:30,720 --> 00:37:34,720
and the man that went on to form Adidas.

637
00:37:34,820 --> 00:37:39,640
Today, it's one of the world's leading sportswear manufacturers.

638
00:37:39,740 --> 00:37:43,340
In the years since, runners have gotten faster and faster,

639
00:37:43,440 --> 00:37:47,340
and some believe it's because of shoe technology.

640
00:37:47,341 --> 00:37:50,240
But Adidas isn't satisfied yet.

641
00:37:50,340 --> 00:37:53,640
It's focusing on bringing down the weight of spikes,

642
00:37:53,740 --> 00:37:57,080
which until recently weighed as much as 250 grams,

643
00:37:57,180 --> 00:37:59,480
or 8.8 ounces.

644
00:37:59,580 --> 00:38:02,380
So this is the Adizero Prime Sprint.

645
00:38:02,480 --> 00:38:07,000
This is the fastest and latest product ever created by Adidas.

646
00:38:07,100 --> 00:38:11,200
Weighing in at only 99 grams, or 3.5 ounces,

647
00:38:11,300 --> 00:38:15,240
the Adizero Prime is the lightest track shoe ever made.

648
00:38:15,241 --> 00:38:19,080
So how did they get from this to this?

649
00:38:19,180 --> 00:38:23,380
75 years of experience and a three-year project led

650
00:38:23,480 --> 00:38:25,980
by innovation director Mick Lussier.

651
00:38:26,080 --> 00:38:29,580
We were trying to do two things when we started this project.

652
00:38:29,680 --> 00:38:32,600
We were trying to make the lightest sprint spike in the

653
00:38:32,700 --> 00:38:36,000
world, and we also tried to make the fastest one.

654
00:38:36,100 --> 00:38:40,900
A challenge this big requires starting in the lab.

655
00:38:41,000 --> 00:38:45,000
Sports researcher Janet Griffin uses multiple technologies

656
00:38:45,001 --> 00:38:47,260
technologies to analyze a shoe's performance.

657
00:38:52,980 --> 00:38:56,240
High-speed video cameras and retro-reflective markers

658
00:38:56,241 --> 00:38:59,340
attached to the athlete allow Janet to study movement.

659
00:39:03,660 --> 00:39:07,080
High-tech scales along the track measure directional forces.

660
00:39:08,220 --> 00:39:11,400
We can measure anything pushing from the side.

661
00:39:11,401 --> 00:39:13,660
you can see a horizontal load,

662
00:39:13,760 --> 00:39:15,740
as well as pushing forward and backward.

663
00:39:17,380 --> 00:39:20,520
Janet wants to make sure the shoes propel the athlete forward.

664
00:39:23,340 --> 00:39:26,260
And so far, the forces are with them.

665
00:39:27,020 --> 00:39:29,820
So the red arrows that come out of these plates

666
00:39:29,821 --> 00:39:30,680
are the force vectors,

667
00:39:31,380 --> 00:39:35,320
and the magnitude, how large of a force,

668
00:39:35,420 --> 00:39:36,760
is the height of the vector.

669
00:39:37,460 --> 00:39:39,820
This is exactly what Janet's been looking for.

670
00:39:39,821 --> 00:39:43,820
And finally, specialized insoles measure the pressure

671
00:39:43,920 --> 00:39:45,660
within the actual shoe.

672
00:39:45,760 --> 00:39:48,560
So throughout here are 99 sensors.

673
00:39:48,660 --> 00:39:52,260
You can kind of see the cabling of the sensors

674
00:39:52,360 --> 00:39:55,360
throughout the insole.

675
00:39:55,460 --> 00:39:57,580
The insole data reveals where the athlete

676
00:39:57,680 --> 00:40:00,080
needs traction support.

677
00:40:00,180 --> 00:40:02,780
So these areas have the highest pressure,

678
00:40:02,880 --> 00:40:05,480
and so those are where we would want to put spikes in the shoe.

679
00:40:05,481 --> 00:40:12,480
With this new design, the team creates another prototype.

680
00:40:12,481 --> 00:40:16,320
And the entire process is done over and over

681
00:40:16,321 --> 00:40:18,320
until they get it just right.

682
00:40:21,320 --> 00:40:24,440
All this work just to design the bottom of a shoe.

683
00:40:26,440 --> 00:40:30,280
At the same time, designer Gabe Laeau faces the challenge

684
00:40:30,281 --> 00:40:32,840
of the upper, or top, of the shoe.

685
00:40:32,841 --> 00:40:34,340
the top of the shoe.

686
00:40:34,341 --> 00:40:36,340
For this shoe, since we're going so lightweight,

687
00:40:36,341 --> 00:40:39,080
you're trying to think of supporting the foot as best

688
00:40:39,081 --> 00:40:42,180
as you can with reducing materials.

689
00:40:42,181 --> 00:40:43,880
Gabe's solution?

690
00:40:43,881 --> 00:40:49,880
Finner laces, shorter raglets, perforated material,

691
00:40:49,881 --> 00:40:54,100
and reduced collar height, all to reduce weight.

692
00:40:54,101 --> 00:40:56,200
Every detail that goes into the design of this shoe

693
00:40:56,201 --> 00:40:57,400
is very thought out.

694
00:40:57,401 --> 00:41:00,200
Everything on this shoe has a reason to be there.

695
00:41:00,201 --> 00:41:03,900
Adi Zero Prime, a state-of-the-art shoe,

696
00:41:03,901 --> 00:41:06,200
tested and designed to the max.

697
00:41:06,201 --> 00:41:08,600
At this level of product, even a couple of grams

698
00:41:08,601 --> 00:41:10,840
could make the difference between winning gold

699
00:41:10,841 --> 00:41:13,580
or being last.

700
00:41:13,581 --> 00:41:17,720
Already, the results speak for themselves.

701
00:41:17,721 --> 00:41:21,320
In 2011, athletes won two world championships

702
00:41:21,321 --> 00:41:22,260
wearing this spike.

703
00:41:27,220 --> 00:41:29,460
Wherever you step, shoes have changed

704
00:41:29,461 --> 00:41:31,260
the course of history.

705
00:41:31,360 --> 00:41:35,460
We've managed to construct an amazing variety of shoes

706
00:41:35,560 --> 00:41:38,560
that allow us to do an amazing variety of things

707
00:41:38,660 --> 00:41:41,200
that we couldn't do otherwise.

708
00:41:41,300 --> 00:41:43,400
Designed and built with purpose,

709
00:41:43,500 --> 00:41:46,220
they protect and enable us in ways not possible

710
00:41:46,320 --> 00:41:48,820
by the bare foot alone.

711
00:41:48,920 --> 00:41:53,620
No matter the type, no matter the size,

712
00:41:53,720 --> 00:41:57,720
every day shoes continue to shape our lives,

713
00:41:57,721 --> 00:42:00,120
making them a modern marvel.

